var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"This page summarizes the public API exported by CodeEdit.jl. For task-oriented examples, see Getting started, Searching source, and Editing code.\n\nMost workflows start by collecting Handles, selecting the blocks to edit, constructing one or more AbstractEdit values, displaying the planned diff, and applying it through an explicit version-control policy.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Handles-and-source-blocks","page":"API reference","text":"Handles are stable references to parsed source or text blocks. They can be created from files, methods, stack frames, repositories, and search results.","title":"Handles and source blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Searching-and-filtering","page":"API reference","text":"Search functions return Set{Handle} values. Use ordinary set operations such as union, intersect, and setdiff to combine selections.","title":"Searching and filtering"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Edits","page":"API reference","text":"Edits are immutable descriptions of intended changes. Constructing an edit does not modify the filesystem. Displaying or stringifying an edit shows the planned diff and records the displayed plan for optional review enforcement.","title":"Edits"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Applying-edits-and-version-control","page":"API reference","text":"Edits are applied through an explicit VersionControl specification. Git-backed application can check cleanliness, require files to be versioned, stage affected paths, and create commits. NoVersionControl is available for scratch files and generated output.","title":"Applying edits and version control"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Handle-utilities","page":"API reference","text":"These convenience functions inspect handles and validate handles or edits.\n\nstring(handle) returns the source text for a handle. string(edit) and display(edit) show the planned diff and mark that exact plan as displayed.","title":"Handle utilities"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.Handle","page":"API reference","text":"Handle(path, line[, pos=1]; parse_as=:auto)\nHandle(method)\nHandle(stackframe)\n\nReference to one parsed source or text block.\n\nThe file constructor loads path and returns the block containing the 1-based (line, pos) location. If the location falls between blocks, the next block is returned; requesting the end-of-file location returns the EOF handle. parse_as may be :auto, :julia, or :text.\n\nMethod and stack-frame constructors use Julia source-location metadata and return an invalid handle when no source location is available. Test handles with is_valid before using them when source information may be missing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.Handle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.handles","page":"API reference","text":"handles(path; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\nhandles(paths; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\nhandles(root, pattern; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\nhandles(repo::VersionControl; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\nhandles(methods_or_stacktrace)\n\nReturn a Set{Handle} for parsed blocks from files, directories, repositories, method lists, or stack traces.\n\nhandles(root, pattern) searches paths matched by Glob.glob(pattern, root). For git-backed VersionControl objects, only tracked UTF-8 files are scanned; NoVersionControl() returns an empty set.\n\nIf includes=true, statically resolvable Julia include(...) calls are followed recursively. parse_as may be :auto, :julia, or :text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.handles"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.handle_at","page":"API reference","text":"handle_at(handles, key)\nhandle_at(handles, path_suffix, line[, pos])\n\nReturn the unique valid handle selected by file suffix and source location.\n\nkey must be \"path:line\" or \"path:line:pos\". path_suffix is matched against the end of each handle's filepath. Without pos, the selected block must touch line; with pos, the block must contain that exact character position on the line.\n\nThrows ArgumentError if no path matches, the path suffix is ambiguous, no block covers the requested location, or multiple blocks match.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.handle_at"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.eof_handle","page":"API reference","text":"eof_handle(path; parse_as=:auto)\n\nReturn a handle to the end-of-file block for path.\n\nThe EOF handle is useful as an insertion target when appending code. parse_as may be :auto, :julia, or :text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.eof_handle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.reindex","page":"API reference","text":"reindex(path)\nreindex()\n\nRefresh cached parses after files changed outside CodeEdit.\n\nreindex(path) reparses one cached file. reindex() reparses all cached files that still exist. Existing handles are preserved only when CodeEdit can match their old block to a unique new block; otherwise they are invalidated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.reindex"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.search","page":"API reference","text":"search(handles, needle)\nsearch(path, needle; parse_as=:auto)\nsearch(paths, needle; parse_as=:auto)\nsearch(root, pattern, needle; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\nsearch(repo::VersionControl, needle)\n\nSearch parsed blocks and return matching handles as a Set{Handle}.\n\nneedle may be a string, matched with occursin, or a Regex. Path arguments are first converted to handles with handles. The (root, pattern) form uses Glob.glob; the repository form searches handles returned by handles(repo).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.search"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.filepath_matches","page":"API reference","text":"filepath_matches(handle, regex)\nfilepath_matches(regex, handle)\nfilepath_matches(regex)\n\nReturn whether handle is valid and its filepath matches regex.\n\nThe one-argument form returns a predicate suitable for filter, search pipelines, and set comprehensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.filepath_matches"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.is_julia","page":"API reference","text":"is_julia(handle)\n\nReturn true if handle is valid and its file was parsed as Julia source.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.is_julia"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.is_text","page":"API reference","text":"is_text(handle)\n\nReturn true if handle is valid and its file was parsed as plain text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.is_text"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.is_versioned","page":"API reference","text":"is_versioned(handle, vc)\nis_versioned(vc, handle)\nis_versioned(vc)\n\nReturn whether handle is valid and its file is tracked by the git-backed version-control policy vc.\n\nFor NoVersionControl(), this always returns false. The one-argument form returns a predicate suitable for filtering handles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.is_versioned"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.AbstractEdit","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractEdit\n\nAbstract supertype for all edit values.\n\nConcrete edits describe source or filesystem changes without performing them. They can be displayed to review the planned diff, validated with is_valid, combined with Combine or *, and applied with apply!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.AbstractEdit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.Replace","page":"API reference","text":"Replace(handle, code)\n\nEdit that replaces the block referenced by handle with code.\n\nThe replacement is planned against the current contents of the handle's file. Applying the edit may invalidate or update handles that refer to affected blocks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.Replace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.Delete","page":"API reference","text":"Delete(handle)\n\nEdit that deletes the block referenced by handle.\n\nDeleting an EOF handle is valid and has no effect.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.Delete"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.InsertBefore","page":"API reference","text":"InsertBefore(handle, code)\n\nEdit that inserts code immediately before the block referenced by handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.InsertBefore"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.InsertAfter","page":"API reference","text":"InsertAfter(handle, code)\n\nEdit that inserts code immediately after the block referenced by handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.InsertAfter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.CreateFile","page":"API reference","text":"CreateFile(path, code; parse_as=:auto)\n\nEdit that creates a new file at path containing code.\n\npath is stored as an absolute path when the edit is constructed. The file must not already exist when the edit is applied. parse_as controls how handles in the new file are parsed and may be :auto, :julia, or :text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.CreateFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.MoveFile","page":"API reference","text":"MoveFile(old_path, new_path)\n\nEdit that moves or renames a file from old_path to new_path.\n\nBoth paths are stored as absolute paths when the edit is constructed. The source must exist and the destination must not exist when the edit is applied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.MoveFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.DeleteFile","page":"API reference","text":"DeleteFile(path)\n\nEdit that deletes the file at path.\n\nThe path is stored as an absolute path when the edit is constructed. Handles for deleted files are invalidated after application.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.DeleteFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.Combine","page":"API reference","text":"Combine(edits...)\nCombine(edits::AbstractVector)\n\nEdit that plans and applies several edits as one operation.\n\nEdits are interpreted in order, so later edits see the virtual filesystem produced by earlier edits. Validation succeeds or fails for the combined plan as a unit. The * operator is shorthand for combining edits in left-to-right order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.Combine"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.displayed!","page":"API reference","text":"displayed!(edit, displayed=true)\n\nSet or clear the displayed-plan marker for edit.\n\nWhen displayed is true, CodeEdit compiles the current edit plan and stores its fingerprint as the plan approved for apply!(...; require_view=true). This does not print the diff; use it only when intentionally bypassing visible review. Passing false clears the marker.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.displayed!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.VersionControl","page":"API reference","text":"VersionControl(path; kwargs...)\nVersionControl(nothing; kwargs...)\n\nVersion-control policy used by apply!.\n\nVersionControl(path) creates a git-backed policy rooted at path (or any path inside the worktree). VersionControl(nothing) creates a no-version-control policy. Keyword arguments are stored as defaults and forwarded to apply!; common options include require_view, require_versioning, require_clean, formatter, and default_message.\n\nPrefer the clearer aliases GitVersionControl and NoVersionControl when the desired backend is known.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.VersionControl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.GitVersionControl","page":"API reference","text":"GitVersionControl(path; kwargs...)\n\nCreate a git-backed version-control policy for the worktree containing path. Keyword arguments are used as default apply! options.\n\nWhen applied with a commit message, git-backed edits check versioning requirements, stage affected paths, and create a commit for the edit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.GitVersionControl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.NoVersionControl","page":"API reference","text":"NoVersionControl(; kwargs...)\n\nCreate a policy that applies edits directly to the filesystem without staging or committing changes. Keyword arguments are used as default apply! options.\n\nThis is useful for scratch files, generated files, and tests. Use NoVersionControl(require_view=true) to still require a displayed diff before writing files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.NoVersionControl"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.apply!","page":"API reference","text":"apply!(vc::VersionControl, edit; kwargs...)\napply!(vc::VersionControl, edit, message; kwargs...)\n\nPlan, validate, and apply edit using the version-control policy vc.\n\nWith GitVersionControl, the message form stages affected paths and creates a git commit when there are staged changes. Without a positional message, default_message must be provided in vc or as a keyword argument. With NoVersionControl, edits are written directly and any positional message is ignored.\n\nImportant keyword arguments:\n\nrequire_view=false: require the edit to have been displayed or stringified, and reject application if the plan changed since review.\nrequire_versioning: require existing files to be tracked by git. Defaults to true for git-backed policies and false otherwise.\nrequire_clean: reject dirty tracked files in scope. Defaults to true unless precommit_message is provided.\nprecommit_message: commit dirty tracked files before applying the edit.\natomic_repo=false: apply dirty-file checks and precommits to the whole repository instead of only affected paths.\nformatter: function from AbstractString to AbstractString run on affected files before and/or after the edit.\npreformat=true: when a formatter is supplied, format before applying so handles can be reindexed against formatted source.\nformat_message: commit message for formatter-only changes in git mode.\ndefault_message: commit message used by git mode when message is omitted.\n\nReturns an ApplyResult summarizing changed files, commits, the applied diff, and paths changed by formatting. Applying an edit may update or invalidate handles that refer to affected files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.apply!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.filepath","page":"API reference","text":"filepath(handle)\n\nReturn the absolute path of the file containing handle.\n\nThrows ArgumentError if the handle is invalid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.filepath"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.lines","page":"API reference","text":"lines(handle)\n\nReturn the 1-based line range covered by handle.\n\nThrows ArgumentError if the handle is invalid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.lines"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.docstring","page":"API reference","text":"docstring(handle)\n\nReturn leading Julia string-literal docstrings attached to handle, or nothing if none are found.\n\nAdjacent leading string literals are joined with newlines. Throws ArgumentError if the handle is invalid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.docstring"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.is_valid","page":"API reference","text":"is_valid(handle)\nis_valid(edit)\n\nReturn whether handle currently refers to a valid parsed block, or whether edit can be planned without validation errors.\n\nHandles may become invalid after file edits, file deletion, or external changes that cannot be matched during reindexing. For edits, this performs planning but does not write to the filesystem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.is_valid"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Blocks-and-handles","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"CodeEdit.jl edits files by first splitting them into source or text blocks. A Handle is a stable reference to one such block and is the object passed to search and edit operations.\n\nEdits built from handles can be applied through git or through an explicit no-version-control specification.","title":"Blocks and handles"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Blocks","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"For Julia files, blocks are top-level syntactic units such as functions, types, macros, constants, assignments, imports, exports, and includes. Attached docstrings stay with the block they document, so replacing a documented function keeps the docstring and function together. Use docstring to inspect the docstring attached to a handle.\n\nFor example, CodeEdit.jl sees a file like this as several separate blocks:\n\nmodule Inventory              # block\n\nconst DEFAULT_TAX = 0.25       # block\n\nfunction price_with_tax(x)     # block\n    return x * (1 + DEFAULT_TAX)\nend\n\nend                           # block\n                              # EOF block\n\nA Julia module is split into separate blocks. The module ... line and its matching end line are separate blocks, while the module body is subdivided normally. Single-line modules are kept as one block. Modules where the beginning or end include more code on the same line, such as module M; x = 1 or y = 2; end # module, also become one single block; avoid this syntax when you want the module body to be editable as separate blocks.\n\nAt the end of each file, CodeEdit.jl creates a special EOF block. EOF handles are useful when inserting code at the end of a file.\n\nFor non-Julia files, blocks are paragraphs separated by blank lines.","title":"Blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Handles","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"A Handle points to one parsed block. It is the object passed to search, display, and edit operations.\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 14)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 13 - 15:\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 1\nend\n\nBecause line 14 is inside increment, the handle refers to the whole increment block.\n\nHandles are interned for a parsed file: requesting the same block again returns the same handle object.\n\njulia> h === Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 13)\ntrue","title":"Handles"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Handle-collections","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"handles and search return sets of handles. A handle appears at most once in a set, so standard set operations are useful for combining and narrowing selections:\n\nrepo = VersionControl(\"examples\")\nhs = handles(repo)\n\ndefinitions = search(hs, \"function\")\nlimits = search(hs, \"DEFAULT_LIMIT\")\ntargets = union(definitions, limits)\n\nSet iteration order is arbitrary. The displayed summary is sorted for readability. Use sort!(collect(hs)) when order matters.\n\nUse a vector when order has meaning. For example, Handle.(stacktrace) preserves stacktrace order, which is useful when inspecting errors.","title":"Handle collections"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Julia-and-text-parsing","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"By default, .jl files are parsed as Julia source and other files are parsed as text. Use parse_as=:julia or parse_as=:text to override this behavior when constructing or collecting handles.\n\njulia> handles(\"examples/notes.txt\"; parse_as=:text)\n3 handles\n# examples/notes.txt:\n  1 - 1: First note.\n  3 - 3: Second note.\n  EOF:\n\nA cached file has one parse mode at a time. Reloading the same file with a different parse mode invalidates existing handles for that file.","title":"Julia and text parsing"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Handle-validity","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"Edits update handles when their referenced block can still be matched after the edit. Handles are invalidated when their block is deleted or can no longer be matched unambiguously.\n\nUse is_valid to test whether a handle still refers to a valid block.\n\njulia> is_valid(h)\ntrue\n\nFiles modified outside CodeEdit.jl are reparsed automatically when a cached timestamp changes. Call reindex to update cached handles explicitly:\n\njulia> reindex(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\");","title":"Handle validity"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Editing-code","page":"Editing code","text":"Edit constructors create immutable edit values. Use apply! to write an edit through an explicit version-control specification.\n\nVersionControl -> handles -> Edit -> displayed plan -> apply!\n\nThe standard workflow uses VersionControl to apply the edit, stage the affected paths, and create a git commit. See Safety and version control for review requirements, validation, and dirty-file behavior.\n\nIn doctest examples, omitting the semicolon from the edit = ... line displays the edit and marks it as displayed. Calling display(edit) has the same effect.\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\nGitVersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\n\njulia> hs = handles(repo);\n","title":"Editing code"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Choosing-an-edit-operation","page":"Editing code","text":"Most edits correspond to one of the following operations:\n\nreplace an existing block with Replace;\ninsert code near an existing block with InsertBefore or InsertAfter;\nappend code with eof_handle and InsertBefore: the EOF handle is a zero-width anchor at the end of the file;\ndelete a block with Delete;\ncreate, move, or delete whole files;\ngroup related edits with Combine.\n\nThe sections below follow that progression.","title":"Choosing an edit operation"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Replacing-a-block","page":"Editing code","text":"A replacement edit changes exactly the block referenced by a handle. This is usually the safest way to update a function, because the planned diff is limited to the selected block.\n\njulia> h = only(search(hs, \"function increment\"))\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 13 - 15:\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 1\nend\n\njulia> new_code = replace(string(h), \"x + 1\" => \"x + 2\");\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, new_code)\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n14c14\n<     return x + 1\n---\n>     return x + 2\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Change increment\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 67decaf\n","title":"Replacing a block"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Inserting-code","page":"Editing code","text":"Insertion edits are useful when a nearby block provides a stable anchor point.\n\nInsert before a block:\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 14)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 13 - 15:\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 2\nend\n\njulia> edit = InsertBefore(h, raw\"\"\"\n       const SCALE = 2\n       \n       \"\"\")\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n12c13,14\n---\n> const SCALE = 2\n>\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add scale constant\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 8c0ffee\n\n\nInsert after a block:\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 16)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 15 - 17:\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 2\nend\n\njulia> edit = InsertAfter(h, raw\"\"\"\n       \n       function scaled_increment(x)\n           return increment(x) * SCALE\n       end\n       \"\"\")\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n18c19,22\n---\n> function scaled_increment(x)\n>     return increment(x) * SCALE\n> end\n>\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add scaled_increment\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit c0ffeed\n\n\nUse raw string literals such as raw\"\"\"...\"\"\" when writing Julia code as strings. They avoid accidental escaping of backslashes and dollar signs.\n\nInserted text is used exactly as provided. Include leading or trailing newlines when you want blank lines around the inserted code.","title":"Inserting code"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Deleting-code","page":"Editing code","text":"julia> h = only(search(handles(repo), \"function obsolete\"))\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 27 - 29:\nfunction obsolete()\n    return :remove_me\nend\n\njulia> edit = Delete(h)\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n27,29c26\n< function obsolete()\n<     return :remove_me\n< end\n---\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Remove obsolete function\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 123cafe\n\n\nDeleting an EOF handle has no effect and is usually not useful.","title":"Deleting code"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Creating,-moving,-and-deleting-files","page":"Editing code","text":"julia> edit = CreateFile(\"examples/generated.jl\", raw\"\"\"\n       function generated_value()\n           return :ok\n       end\n       \"\"\")\nEdit creates examples/generated.jl:\n0c1,3\n---\n> function generated_value()\n>     return :ok\n> end\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add generated file\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 1bada55\n\n\njulia> edit = MoveFile(\"examples/generated.jl\", \"examples/generated-renamed.jl\")\nEdit moves examples/generated.jl -> examples/generated-renamed.jl\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Rename generated file\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 2facade\n\n\njulia> edit = DeleteFile(\"examples/generated-renamed.jl\")\nEdit deletes examples/generated-renamed.jl\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Remove generated file\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit deada55\n","title":"Creating, moving, and deleting files"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Combining-edits","page":"Editing code","text":"Use Combine, or the * shorthand, when multiple edits are part of one logical change and should be planned together:\n\njulia> source = only(search(handles(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\"), \"function old_function_name\"))\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 23 - 25:\nfunction old_function_name()\n    return foo(1)\nend\n\njulia> destination = eof_handle(\"examples/notes.txt\")\n# examples/notes.txt EOF:\n\njulia> edit = Combine(\n           InsertBefore(destination, \"\\nMoved selected source:\\n\\n\" * string(source)),\n           Delete(source),\n       )\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n23,25c22\n< function old_function_name()\n<     return foo(1)\n< end\n---\nEdit modifies examples/notes.txt:\n3c4,9\n---\n>\n> Moved selected source:\n>\n> function old_function_name()\n>     return foo(1)\n> end\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Move selected source to notes\")\nApplied: 2 files changed, commit 12feed3\n\n\nEquivalent shorthand:\n\njulia> h = only(search(handles(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\"), \"function increment\"))\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 15 - 17:\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 2\nend\n\njulia> edit = InsertAfter(h, raw\"\"\"\n       \n       function bounded_increment(x)\n           return min(increment(x), DEFAULT_LIMIT)\n       end\n       \"\"\") * InsertBefore(eof_handle(\"examples/notes.txt\"), \"\\nAdded bounded_increment to DemoPackage.jl\\n\")\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n18c19,22\n---\n> function bounded_increment(x)\n>     return min(increment(x), DEFAULT_LIMIT)\n> end\n>\nEdit modifies examples/notes.txt:\n9c10,11\n---\n>\n> Added bounded_increment to DemoPackage.jl\n\n\nCombined edits are validated after the full combined result is planned. Intermediate states may therefore be invalid Julia syntax, provided the final result is valid.\n\nPlanning and validation are all-or-nothing. Applying a combined edit that touches multiple files is still best-effort at the filesystem level: if a later filesystem operation fails, earlier operations may already have been applied. Use version control so changes can be reviewed and recovered.","title":"Combining edits"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Applying-edits-without-version-control","page":"Editing code","text":"For scratch files, generated files, or other changes that should not create a commit, pass an explicit NoVersionControl specification.\n\nNoVersionControl makes uncommitted edits explicit at the call site.\n\njulia> write(\"scratch-note.txt\", \"status = old\\n\")\n13\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"scratch-note.txt\", 1; parse_as=:text)\n# scratch-note.txt 1 - 1:\nstatus = old\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, \"status = new\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch-note.txt:\n1c1\n< status = old\n---\n> status = new\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nApplied: 1 file changed\n","title":"Applying edits without version control"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"This chapter introduces the basic CodeEdit.jl workflow: choose a version-control context, collect handles, find a block, construct an edit, review the plan, and apply it.\n\n(In the examples, we use a small git repository in a directory examples. The file docs/meta_setup.jl creates this repo.)","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Installation","page":"Getting started","text":"Install CodeEdit.jl with Julia's package manager. The package is not yet registered, so add it from its repository URL:\n\npkg> add https://github.com/perrutquist/CodeEdit.jl","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Loading-the-package","page":"Getting started","text":"julia> using CodeEdit","title":"Loading the package"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Creating-a-repository-context","page":"Getting started","text":"For source edits in a git repository, start with VersionControl:\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\nGitVersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\n\nThe same repo object is used to collect editable handles and to apply edits later.","title":"Creating a repository context"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Listing-and-searching-handles","page":"Getting started","text":"Collect parsed source blocks from the repository with handles:\n\njulia> hs = handles(repo);\n\njulia> matches = search(hs, \"old_function_name\")\n1 handle\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n  17 - 19: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n\nSearch results are handles. A handle can be displayed, converted to source text, or passed to an edit constructor:\n\njulia> h = only(search(hs, \"function foo\"))\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 7 - 11:\nfunction foo(x)\n    y = helper(x)\n    z = y * 2\n    return z\nend\n\njulia> source = string(h)\n\"function foo(x)\\n    y = helper(x)\\n    z = y * 2\\n    return z\\nend\\n\"\n\nYou can also look up a unique handle in a collection by filepath suffix and source line with handle_at, or equivalently by indexing with a path:line key:\n\njulia> hs[\"DemoPackage.jl:7\"]\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 7 - 11:\nfunction foo(x)\n    y = helper(x)\n    z = y * 2\n    return z\nend\n\n\nDirect construction with Handle is useful when you already have a file and line number:\n\njulia> Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 10)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 7 - 11:\nfunction foo(x)\n    y = helper(x)\n    z = y * 2\n    return z\nend\n\n\nwarning: Warning\nDo not rely on a displayed line number to create a handle if edits have been applied to that file after the line number was displayed.\n\nnote: Note\nHandles that are obtained via a line number keep referring to the same block, even if the block moves within the file after the handle was created.\n\nSee Searching source for glob searches, regex searches, recursive include traversal, and set operations on handle collections.","title":"Listing and searching handles"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Applying-an-edit-with-git","page":"Getting started","text":"Inspecting handles leaves files unchanged. To change source, construct an edit value and apply it through the repository.\n\nWith require_view=true, displaying the edit records the exact plan. When apply! runs, CodeEdit.jl plans the edit again and refuses to apply it if the current plan differs from the displayed one:\n\nnote: Note\nIn the REPL, evaluating an edit without a trailing semicolon displays it. Calling display(edit) is equivalent.\n\njulia> h = only(search(hs, \"old_function_name\"));\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, replace(string(h), \"old_function_name\" => \"new_function_name\"))\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n17c17\n< function old_function_name()\n---\n> function new_function_name()\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Rename old_function_name\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 3630f3e\n\nThe edit is written to disk and committed to git with the message you provide.","title":"Applying an edit with git"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Inserting-at-the-end-of-a-file","page":"Getting started","text":"Use eof_handle when inserting new code at the end of a file:\n\njulia> h = eof_handle(\"examples/helpers.jl\");\n\njulia> edit = InsertBefore(h, raw\"\"\"\n       \n       another_helper(x) = helper(x) * 2\n       \"\"\")\nEdit modifies examples/helpers.jl:\n1c2,3\n---\n>\n> another_helper(x) = helper(x) * 2\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add another helper\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit c58b1c4","title":"Inserting at the end of a file"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Applying-without-version-control","page":"Getting started","text":"For generated files, scratch files, or other changes that should not create a commit, pass an explicit NoVersionControl specification:\n\njulia> write(\"scratch.txt\", \"temporary = false\\n\");\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"scratch.txt\", 1; parse_as=:text);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, \"temporary = true\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch.txt:\n1c1\n< temporary = false\n---\n> temporary = true\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nApplied: 1 file changed","title":"Applying without version control"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Finding-errors-from-stacktraces","page":"Finding errors from a stacktrace","text":"CodeEdit.jl can locate source blocks referenced by a stacktrace. This makes a debugging session into an editing workflow: catch the error, capture the stacktrace with catch_backtrace(), map frames to handles, inspect the matching blocks, then edit the source and commit the fix.","title":"Finding errors from stacktraces"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Starting-from-a-stacktrace","page":"Finding errors from a stacktrace","text":"Suppose the following call throws an exception:\n\nfunction inner(x)\n    error(\"bad input: $x\")\nend\n\nfunction outer(x)\n    return inner(x + 1)\nend\n\nThe thrown error contains stack frames for both functions:\n\njulia> outer(1)\nERROR: bad input: 2\nStacktrace:\n [1] error(s::String)\n   @ Base ./error.jl:44\n [2] inner(x::Int64)\n   @ Main .../docs/examples/error-example.jl:2\n [3] outer(x::Int64)\n   @ Main .../docs/examples/error-example.jl:6\n\nCapture the backtrace in a variable:\n\njulia> trace = try\n           outer(1)\n       catch\n           stacktrace(catch_backtrace())\n       end;","title":"Starting from a stacktrace"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Inspecting-the-most-relevant-blocks","page":"Finding errors from a stacktrace","text":"Let's display the blocks in our code that appear in the trace, preserving stacktrace order.\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true);\n\njulia> hs = handles(repo);\n\njulia> trace_handles = [h for h in Handle.(trace) if h in hs];\n\njulia> for h in trace_handles\n           println(h)\n       end\n# examples/error-example.jl 1 - 3:\nfunction inner(x)\n    error(\"bad input: $x\")\nend\n\n# examples/error-example.jl 5 - 7:\nfunction outer(x)\n    return inner(x + 1)\nend\n\n\nA displayed handle includes the file name and line range, followed by the source block. Handle.(trace) returns a vector, so the order of stack frames is preserved. By contrast, handles and search return sets, which remove duplicate handles and have arbitrary iteration order.","title":"Inspecting the most relevant blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Editing-after-locating-the-error","page":"Finding errors from a stacktrace","text":"After finding the relevant block, construct a replacement and apply it through git:\n\njulia> h = only(search(trace_handles, \"error(\"))\n# examples/error-example.jl 1 - 3:\nfunction inner(x)\n    error(\"bad input: $x\")\nend\n\njulia> fixed = replace(string(h), \"error(\\\"bad input: \\$x\\\")\" => \"throw(ArgumentError(\\\"bad input: \\$x\\\"))\");\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, fixed)\nEdit modifies examples/error-example.jl:\n2c2\n<     error(\"bad input: $x\")\n---\n>     throw(ArgumentError(\"bad input: $x\"))\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Throw ArgumentError for bad input\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 751de5e\n\nAfter a successful edit, existing handles are updated or invalidated as needed.\n\nFor ordinary string, regex, glob, and recursive include searches, see Searching source.","title":"Editing after locating the error"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Searching-source","page":"Searching source","text":"Searches operate on parsed blocks. Results are handles that can be displayed, inspected, filtered, combined, or passed to edit constructors.\n\nThe usual pattern is:\n\nVersionControl -> handles -> search -> inspect -> edit","title":"Searching source"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Searching-a-repository","page":"Searching source","text":"Create a version-control context, collect handles with handles, then search them by string:\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true);\n\njulia> hs = handles(repo);\n\njulia> search(hs, \"old_function_name\")\n1 handle\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n  17 - 19: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n\nDisplay a match to see the full block:\n\njulia> h = only(search(hs, \"old_function_name\"));\n\njulia> println(h)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 17 - 19:\nfunction old_function_name()\n    return foo(1)\nend\n","title":"Searching a repository"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Regex-searches","page":"Searching source","text":"Use a regular expression when the exact text is not known:\n\njulia> search(hs, r\"function .*increment\")\n1 handle\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n  13 - 15: function increment(x); return x + 1; end","title":"Regex searches"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Searching-files-and-directories","page":"Searching source","text":"You can also collect handles directly from a file or from files matching a glob:\n\njulia> file_handles = handles(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\");\n\njulia> hs = handles(\"examples\", \"*.jl\")\n14 handles\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n   1 -  1: module DemoPackage\n   3 -  3: include(\"helpers.jl\")\n   5 -  5: const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n   7 - 11: function foo(x); y = helper(x); z = y * …\n  13 - 15: function increment(x); return x + 1; end\n  17 - 19: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n  21 - 23: function obsolete(); return :remove_me; …\n  25 - 25: end\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/error-example.jl:\n  1 - 3: function inner(x); error(\"bad input: $x\"…\n  5 - 7: function outer(x); return inner(x + 1); …\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/helpers.jl:\n  1 - 1: helper(x) = x + 1\n  EOF:\n\nFor common cases, search can collect handles and search in one call:\n\njulia> search(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", \"DEFAULT_LIMIT\")\n1 handle\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n  5 - 5: const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10","title":"Searching files and directories"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Following-includes","page":"Searching source","text":"For Julia entry-point files, pass includes=true to follow include statements recursively:\n\njulia> handles(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\"; includes = true)\n11 handles\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n   1 -  1: module DemoPackage\n   3 -  3: include(\"helpers.jl\")\n   5 -  5: const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n   7 - 11: function foo(x); y = helper(x); z = y * …\n  13 - 15: function increment(x); return x + 1; end\n  17 - 19: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n  21 - 23: function obsolete(); return :remove_me; …\n  25 - 25: end\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/helpers.jl:\n  1 - 1: helper(x) = x + 1\n  EOF:\n\nRecursive include traversal uses cycle detection, so include loops are visited at most once.","title":"Following includes"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Handle-sets-and-order","page":"Searching source","text":"handles and search return sets. A handle appears at most once in a set, and standard set operations can be used to combine search results:\n\nfunctions = search(hs, \"function\")\nlimits = search(hs, \"DEFAULT_LIMIT\")\ntargets = union(functions, limits)\n\nThe displayed summary is grouped by file and sorted for readability. Iterating over a set yields arbitrary order. If order matters, use sort!(collect(hs)) to produce a vector in display order.\n\nUse a vector when the input order has meaning. For example, Handle.(stacktrace) preserves stacktrace order; see Finding errors from stacktraces.","title":"Handle sets and order"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Extracting-a-single-handle-from-a-search","page":"Searching source","text":"A search or filter operation that returns a single handle can be passed to the only function to extract that handle, for example:\n\njulia> h0 = only(search(hs, \"function foo\"))\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 7 - 11:\nfunction foo(x)\n    y = helper(x)\n    z = y * 2\n    return z\nend\n\n\nA simple way is often to use the Handle constructor with the filename and line number.\n\njulia> h1 = hs[\"DemoPackage.jl:10\"];\n\njulia> h2 = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 10);\n\njulia> h3 = only(filter(h -> occursin(\"DemoPackage\", filepath(h)) && 10 in lines(h), hs));\n\njulia> h0 == h1 === h2 === h3\ntrue\n","title":"Extracting a single handle from a search"},{"category":"section","location":"#CodeEdit.jl","page":"Home","text":"CodeEdit.jl provides tools for making source edits from Julia. \n\nThe core idea is to divide Julia code into syntactically independent blocks, that can be manipulated directly from Julia.\n\nJulia code frequently consists of fairly short top-level blocks where the meaning of each code block depends very little on its location in relation to other code. It therefore makes sense to be able to access, search and modify code in terms of blocks, rather than files.","title":"CodeEdit.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Manual","page":"Home","text":"Getting started\nBlocks and handles\nSearching source\nEditing code\nSafety and version control\nFinding errors from stacktraces\nAPI reference","title":"Manual"},{"category":"section","location":"#Basic-workflow","page":"Home","text":"A Handle identifies a parsed source block, edit constructors such as Replace and InsertAfter describe changes, and apply! writes the result through an explicit version-control specification.\n\nA common workflow is:\n\nChoose a VersionControl context.\nCollect handles.\nSearch or select blocks.\nConstruct an edit.\nCall apply!.\n\nThe following example changes one function in a repository, applies the edit, and reads the edited block back from disk.\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\nGitVersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\n\njulia> hs = handles(repo);\n\njulia> h = only(search(hs, \"function increment\"))\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 13 - 15:\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 1\nend\n\njulia> replacement = replace(string(h), \"x + 1\" => \"x + 2\");\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, replacement)\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n14c14\n<     return x + 1\n---\n>     return x + 2\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Change increment\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 4c0ffee\n\njulia> println(string(Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 14)));\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 2\nend\n\nIf Revise.jl is loaded, CodeEdit.jl calls Revise after a successful edit, so changed method definitions usually take effect immediately.\n\nSee Blocks and handles for the source model and Safety and version control for review, validation, and git behavior.","title":"Basic workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Safety-and-version-control","page":"Safety and version control","text":"CodeEdit.jl separates planning from application. Constructing an edit value describes a change; applying an edit writes it to the filesystem.\n\nDisplaying an edit shows the planned change. Applying the edit replans it, checks that the result is valid, writes files, and, in the standard workflow, records the change as a git commit.","title":"Safety and version control"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Planning-before-applying","page":"Safety and version control","text":"An edit such as Replace, InsertBefore, or Delete describes an intended change. It can be inspected before it is applied:\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 5)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 5 - 5:\nconst DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n\njulia> new_source = replace(string(handle), \"10\" => \"20\")\n\"const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 20\\n\"\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, new_source)\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n5c5\n< const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n---\n> const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 20\n\nWhen require_view=true, CodeEdit.jl stores the exact plan that was displayed. Later, apply! plans the edit again and refuses to apply it if the current plan differs from the displayed plan.\n\nThis protects against applying a stale edit after the surrounding file has changed.\n\nFor example, this edit is displayed against the original file, but the file is changed before apply! runs:\n\njulia> write(\"scratch.txt\", \"status = old\\n\");\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"scratch.txt\", 1; parse_as=:text);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, \"status = new\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch.txt:\n1c1\n< status = old\n---\n> status = new\n\njulia> write(\"scratch.txt\", \"status = changed elsewhere\\n\");\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nERROR: displayed edit was invalid\n\nDisplay the edit again to review the current plan before applying it.","title":"Planning before applying"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Git-backed-editing","page":"Safety and version control","text":"The standard workflow starts with VersionControl:\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true);\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 5);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, replace(string(handle), \"10\" => \"20\"))\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n5c5\n< const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n---\n> const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 20\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Update default limit\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 0000000\n\nA git-backed apply writes the edited files, stages the affected paths, and creates a commit. By default, CodeEdit.jl expects edited files to be tracked by git and rejects creation outside the worktree.\n\nGit is the recommended undo and recovery mechanism for CodeEdit.jl edits.","title":"Git-backed editing"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Dirty-files","page":"Safety and version control","text":"CodeEdit.jl can reject edits when relevant tracked files are dirty. This prevents an edit from accidentally mixing with uncommitted changes in the same files.\n\nIf you deliberately want to checkpoint existing dirty work first, supply precommit_message. CodeEdit.jl can then commit the existing changes before formatting or applying the new edit. This supports two workflows:\n\nkeep the relevant files clean before each edit;\nexplicitly checkpoint dirty work before CodeEdit.jl changes anything.\n\nThe important rule is to make the state of the worktree intentional before applying an edit. Do not rely on CodeEdit.jl as an undo stack; use git history for review and recovery.","title":"Dirty files"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Applying-without-version-control","page":"Safety and version control","text":"For scratch files, generated files, or temporary changes, use NoVersionControl:\n\njulia> write(\"scratch.txt\", \"temporary = false\\n\");\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"scratch.txt\", 1; parse_as=:text);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, \"temporary = true\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch.txt:\n1c1\n< temporary = false\n---\n> temporary = true\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nApplied: 1 file changed\n\nThis mode makes uncommitted edits explicit at the call site.","title":"Applying without version control"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Validation","page":"Safety and version control","text":"Julia files are reparsed before edits are applied. If the final result would introduce syntax errors, the edit is rejected before the invalid source is written:\n\njulia> write(\"scratch.jl\", \"function ok()\\n    return 1\\nend\\n\");\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"scratch.jl\", 1);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, \"function broken(\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch.jl:\n1,3c1\n< function ok()\n<     return 1\n< end\n---\n> function broken(\nValidation errors:\n- scratch.jl has a Julia syntax error\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nERROR: displayed edit was invalid\n\nCombined edits are planned and validated as a unit, so intermediate states may be invalid as long as the final result is valid.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Combined-edits-and-filesystem-failures","page":"Safety and version control","text":"Combine lets several edits be planned together. Planning and validation are all-or-nothing.\n\nApplying a combined edit that touches multiple files is still best-effort at the filesystem level. If an early file operation succeeds and a later one fails, the filesystem can be left partially changed.\n\nUse git-backed editing for source changes you want to review, commit, or recover.","title":"Combined edits and filesystem failures"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Limitations","page":"Safety and version control","text":"CodeEdit.jl rejects edits when it cannot replan or validate them safely. Use version control for history, review, and recovery.\n\nIn particular:\n\nmulti-file applies are not atomic at the filesystem level;\nno built-in undo stack is provided;\nhandles can become invalid when their referenced blocks are deleted or can no longer be matched;\nno-version-control edits are not committed unless you commit them yourself.\n\nThe safest workflow is to work in a git repository, require review for edits that matter, and keep each change small.","title":"Limitations"}]
}
